To solve the equation $(2x+1)(3x+2) = (x-3)(6x-5)$, first we need to expand both sides of the equation. \begin{align*} (2x+1)(3x+2) &= (x-3)(6x-5). \\ 6x^2 + 4x + 3x + 2 &= 6x^2 - 5x - 18x + 15. \\ 6x^2 + 7x + 2 &= 6x^2 - 23x + 15. \end{align*} Now we can subtract $6x^2$ from the left and right sides, and then subtract $-23x$ and $+15$ from the right side as well: \begin{align*} 6x^2 + 7x + 2 - \boxed{6x^2} &= 6x^2 - 23x + 15 - \boxed{6x^2} - (-23x) - +15 \\ 7x + 2 &= -23x +15. \end{align*} Next, we move the $7x$ and $-23x$ from the other side to the side on which they are negative, and add $-2$ to both sides: \begin{align*} 7x +2 \underbrace{-(7x - 23x)} &= -23x +15 \underbrace{- (7x - 23x)} -2 \\ 30x + 2 &= -23x +97. \\ 30x -2 &= \underbrace{-23x + 23x} +97 -2. \\ 30x -2 &= 95. \\ 30x &= \overbrace{95+2}^{95 -2 = 93}. \\ 30x &= 95. \\ x &= \frac{95}{30}. \\ x &= \frac{19}{6}. \end{align*}
