 label the sides of the grid corresponding to units on the X and Y axis. Then the area of each given rectangle has to be 3 units, i.e., base $\times$ height=3. Now, there are lots of such rectangles, formed with different lengths of bases and heights. <expand>One example of such a rectangle is formed on the square whose sides are parallel to the sides of the given bounding rectangle and whose length of two sides is 1 and 3 units respectively. <tex>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node[anchor=south east,inner sep=0] (A) {}; 
\node[anchor=south east,inner sep=0] (B) [45mm below of=A] {};
\node[anchor=south east,inner sep=0] (C) [1.25cm right of=B] {};
\node[anchor=south west] at (A) {3};
\node[anchor=south west] at (B) {1};
\node[anchor=southwest,inner sep=0] at (C) {3};
\draw[fill=red] (0,1) rectangle (3,2);
\end{tikzpicture}
.

Label the sides of each rectangle in the given figure.</tex></expand> Now, in other examples of such rectangles, the length of either the base or height is a multiple of 3, but the length of other one is 1 because they would cross different grid line segments. <expand>As shown above, if we extend any vertical side of the grid below a rectangle with base 1, we can draw one more line segment on X axis parallel to the given height 3 of this rectangle and get a second unit from grid lines. In the same way, we can extend any horizontal side of grid on top of any rectangle and get a width 1 line segment parallel to the width 3 of the given one. Such a grid line extension is shown below for the particular rectangle above (and a tiny bit down).<tex>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node[anchor=south east,inner sep=0] (A) {}; 
\node[anchor=south east,inner sep=0] (B) [45mm below of=A] {};
\draw (0,1) rectangle (3,2);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (A + (1,-0.33));
\draw[dashed] (B) -- (B + (0.33,-1));
\end{tikzpicture}

As the dotted lines are drawn parallel to the edges of the given rectangle, they cross 3 corner points, and the vertical dotted line also crosses 1 corner point and the horizontal dotted line also crosses 1 corner point on the parallel horizontal and vertical edges of the given rectangle, respectively.</tex>
Doing this technique on all the rectangles of area 3 square units in all squares of this given grid you will get that there is 14 of such rectangles. And hence the final answer is 14 rectangles.</expand>  </answer>

