In the complex plane, the equation of a line segment joining two complex numbers is
\begin{align*}
z(t) = x_0 + (z_1-z_0)i
\end{align*}
where $z_0=x_0 + y_0 i$ is the starting point or, the origin, and $z_1=x_1+yi$ is the endpoint. We are looking for all $z(t)$ such that $0<t<1$ and gap is $t$ between the two complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_0$.


Since the complex plane is isomorphic to the Euclidean plane, the power of a complex number can be measured by using the (ordinary) sum-of-the-squares of the real and imaginary parts. Then there is an isomorphism from the complex plane into the Euclidean plane. This means that, using the result of the picture, fields, babies of algebra, and other mathematics, we have proved the distance between two complex numbers. Since
\begin{align*}
\text{distance}(z_0, z_1)=\sqrt{(x_1-x_0)^2 + (y_1-y_0)^2}
\end{align*}
in the Euclidean plane, substituting $z_0=x_0 + y_0 i$ and $z_1=x_1+yi$, where $i=\sqrt{-1}$, we have shown that the distance between two complex numbers $z_0$ and $z_1$ is simply the Euclidean distance between their corresponding points. Notice that it is quite similar to the equation for a line segment in the Euclidean plane.

